How functions work in Rust: fn, parameters with types, and the → return type, explained line by line. (Rust Day 9 of the Zero to Pro series)

📌 In this short:
✅ fn name(a: i32, b: i32) → i32: every parameter needs a type, the arrow gives the return type
✅ The last expression without a semicolon is the return value (no return keyword needed)
✅ Forget the → return type and the compiler tells you exactly how to fix it (E0308)

💻 Code from the video (YouTube won't show the real arrow: type → as a minus sign then a greater-than sign):
fn main() {
    let sum = add(2, 3);
    println!("sum = {sum}");
}

fn add(a: i32, b: i32) → i32 {
    a + b
}

📖 Learn more: https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch03-03-how-functions-work.html
